fornthulani-plugin-msgpack-body
===============================

MessagePack body parsing for the
[fornthulani](https://crates.io/crates/fornthulani) micro HTTP framework.

Adds one method to every `Request`:

    req.msgpack::<T>()  ->  Result<T, ThulaniError>


----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSTALL
----------------------------------------------------------------------

  [dependencies]
  fornthulani = "0.1.0"
  fornthulani-plugin-msgpack-body = "0.1.0"


----------------------------------------------------------------------
USE
----------------------------------------------------------------------

  use serde::Deserialize;
  use fornthulani::{Params, Request, Response, Status, ThulaniError};
  use fornthulani_plugin_msgpack_body::MsgPackBody;

  #[derive(Deserialize)]
  struct NewUser {
      name: String,
      email: String,
  }

  fn create_user(req: &Request, _: &Params) -> Result<Response, ThulaniError> {
      let input: NewUser = req.msgpack()?;
      Ok(Response::json(Status::CREATED, &input))
  }

Register the controller the usual way:

  router.post("/users", create_user);


----------------------------------------------------------------------
BEHAVIOUR
----------------------------------------------------------------------

  Content-Type not application/msgpack or application/x-msgpack
      ->  Err(ThulaniError::BadRequest) with 400
          "expected Content-Type: application/msgpack, got `<ct>`"

  Empty body
      ->  Err(ThulaniError::BadRequest) with 400
          "empty body"

  Body is not valid MessagePack
      ->  Err(ThulaniError::BadRequest) with 400
          "invalid msgpack: <parse error>"

  Otherwise
      ->  Ok(T)


----------------------------------------------------------------------
TESTING
----------------------------------------------------------------------

  cargo test

Three integration tests, all passing end-to-end through the server:

  accepts_valid_msgpack
  rejects_wrong_content_type
  rejects_invalid_msgpack

The doc-test in src/lib.rs is compiled (no_run) so the usage pattern
above is validated on every `cargo test`.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
DESIGN
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Like its JSON sibling, this crate does NOT implement
fornthulani::Middleware.

Body parsing is a controller-side capability: it only runs when a
controller asks for it with `req.msgpack()`. Middleware is for
lifecycle hooks (auth, logging, CORS). Keeping the two separate means:

  - No runtime cost unless a handler calls `.msgpack()`.
  - Zero changes to fornthulani core.
  - 30 lines of code you can read in a minute.

The plugin uses the `path` + `version` form in Cargo.toml:

  fornthulani = { path = "../fornthulani", version = "0.1.0" }

Cargo uses the path when building locally and the version when
publishing, which is the correct shape for a publishable crate whose
library is developed in a sibling folder.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
COMPANION PLUGINS
----------------------------------------------------------------------

  fornthulani-plugin-json-body      req.json::<T>()
  fornthulani-plugin-msgpack-body   req.msgpack::<T>()   (this crate)

Future plugins in the same family:

  fornthulani-plugin-cors           CORS middleware
  fornthulani-plugin-auth           auth middleware
  fornthulani-plugin-logging        request/response logging
  fornthulani-plugin-multipart      multipart/form-data bodies
  fornthulani-plugin-query          query string parsing

Each lives in its own repository and publishes independently.
None of them touch fornthulani/src/.


